er/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437532} :answer-field/id #uuid "8669b21d-34a9-51aa-abeb-f8498fec2f92" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b86aaf3-f04f-5a9d-8491-875b7ee69bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-108605" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dca237f8-afbe-53d7-93eb-c1a93103fb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461629} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8faa15-ce34-56ed-b0e4-86ef723