swer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464233} :answer-field/id #uuid "1242e42f-6c60-532e-9b28-0bc88336c0cd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bb3b4f0-a153-5b1f-93aa-f136a6132e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-141751" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d5ea7e13fa03ac553893aa78aa7ee32ac7b67f2eb19dff32228d53bf2010aa2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049011 :topic/id #uuid "d33f7a90-b31c-5a5d-bdc7-84de678b50eb" :topic/math-academy-id 363 :topic/title "Intervals of Concavity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e345dfe3-6cec-5c47-9653-0c664922f95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464234} {:answer/id #uuid "88876309-8b8f-5f37-af60-4ca60812f2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464235}] :answer