-ca89c9343a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467326} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca2e0c7-307a-569b-a613-32617eec1ceb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2aec92e-c3f4-5cb5-bae0-799d4d7e0d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-35600" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec79ffa15b3ed25d8f439f575e1cdffc3122ae1d82e1b55500a6e5e2e17cf4fc.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace.  Every subspace must contain the origin,  but from the diagram, we can easily see that the origin is not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$  without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049731 :topic/id #uuid "0f127f4a-3cf5-5736-90b8-9817b73eb050" :topic/math-academy-id 1889 :topic/title "Invertible Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca90ac0-fdc1-56ec-bb51-072e1c20d1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438220} {:answer