text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473092} {:answer/id #uuid "474e300d-b0ad-520c-b70a-93c1e5e2e177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9299db-52e0-589b-b002-e37948bbdadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473092} :answer-field/id #uuid "19219c0d-23df-589d-82c4-a37e066f5d0f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35574" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d5d9586684c8606ca0f6df8dd70d9828ea04d3d9097b5a155ab66bcd7300ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case we also have that the vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048805 :topic/id #uuid "c10b84fd-2e2a-576b-a2ce-4b2fb0bbd333" :topic/math-academy-id 4820 :topic/title "Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29f9cbb-000d-5b8c-9f6c-2b45ca8f9c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472898} {:answer/id #uuid "bb936d2a-d218-5632-94f0-f74248519d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb936d2a-d218-5632-94f0-f74248519d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472899} :answer-field/id #uuid "61b2f428-c732-5a03-845e-71d541cb66c5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e88f6e62-a168-5578-bc6f-cde1ddbb2379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472900} {:answer/id #uuid "01a0001b-a449-59f4-9d85-6090aa040780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e88f6e62-a168-5578-bc6f-cde1ddbb2379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472900} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cb8e60-7f3f-5cab-a5a8-f9261791e5bd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473845} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cff02f99-4992-5465-bf2c-8b903daf43f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472902} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6fc2e4-b2f4-5892-9fc2-d6d8f7bc0bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b6fc2e4-b2f4-5892-9fc2-d6d8f7bc0bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472903} :answer-field/id #uuid "af3b864c-298a-50d4-be87-b47b4870ee67" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1a27c07-b36b-5525-ba6b-847b13ebf516" :question/math-academy-id "q-213228" :question/problem "![](images/03/03ddf06402ad38708d232cf28783c817bf881965d54650eb158e7ffca4ea76a2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493 :topic/id #uuid "4db2b54f-79ed-566a-a082-ccbcb95e93a8" :topic/math-academy-id 4821 :topic/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc5f5af-b932-595b-9f45-d7822f805699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472928} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d188d8-7801-5c0a-9e1e-a2aa6485adf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d188d8-7801-5c0a-9e1e-a2aa6485adf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472929} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1ff480-810f-507d-a2d1-a039d0e78424" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32592e22-95ce-53db-acfa-46e6ac2385e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472930} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca0a8a2-f51f-5ad7-800d-598564f182ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32592e22-95ce-53db-acfa-46e6ac2385e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472930} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeaf136c-cb01-591e-bfe6-c7fd00a377a8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a592fe-3124-53ec-b419-b1008565d345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472932} {:answer/id #uuid "15a5c949-7bc0-58e4-a982-02954107b625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a592fe-3124-53ec-b419-b1008565d345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472932} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbec2c6-432a-5be0-b414-344192c413ec" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213318" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493 :topic/id #uuid "4db2b54f-79ed-566a-a082-ccbcb95e93a8" :topic/math-academy-id 4821 :topic/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b72c5fe-7cf1-5d4c-8244-863dd48fbd1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472946} {:answer/id #uuid "a56406c4-8dd3-577e-9eeb-8b1dd58a4181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b72c5fe-7cf1-5d4c-8244-863dd48fbd1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472946} :answer-field/id #uuid "10e53a65-986c-5958-99b5-5f4a013df2f8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adc2b2c3-99e6-5873-938c-abe360fed0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472948} {:answer/id #uuid "6d05c6a6-6c94-5079-9dc8-1d06f4cfa059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d05c6a6-6c94-5079-9dc8-1d06f4cfa059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472949} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f04fe0d-c9ba-5fb1-b2a5-dee9ef3c2597" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473869} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbc29c4-0f7d-531f-babc-73e9e8ec5022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472950} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c8355b-ea42-5d1a-9524-335addcefe34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dbc29c4-0f7d-531f-babc-73e9e8ec5022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472950} :answer-field/id #uuid "60efff53-6597-5c41-9085-3dc22abcbc74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213492" :question/problem "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493 :topic/id #uuid "4db2b54f-79ed-566a-a082-ccbcb95e93a8" :topic/math-academy-id 4821 :topic/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45743ad5-6ebc-573e-9a94-e0395e624eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472958} {:answer/id #uuid "63d7494e-efff-55f7-9f7b-0bb932cb30dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45743ad5-6ebc-573e-9a94-e0395e624eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472958} :answer-field/id #uuid "b386422c-75ef-561b-ae4f-99269f54a065" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f15345a-c110-501d-a15e-6ca892a30197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472960} {:answer/id #uuid "530a3871-c587-5470-b6d5-a754a40d489a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "530a3871-c587-5470-b6d5-a754a40d489a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472961} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb9902a3-28fa-531a-ad17-9f5be891c276" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d3206f5-60dc-5e8e-9b96-56be36aaf3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472962} {:answer/id #uuid "14a6cd09-f28b-53aa-af9f-2c14771bcdce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d3206f5-60dc-5e8e-9b96-56be36aaf3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472962} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72b81df-1a34-5370-bcbb-bc9229da9345" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd780cfc-a5dd-5c8f-a2c7-e44b9d2a3075" :question/math-academy-id "q-213543" :question/problem "![](images/da/dac9030db59b2b51b2b95c8bc9b5988106be8d198b7f947d33c8678d1cfa3b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493 :topic/id #uuid "4db2b54f-79ed-566a-a082-ccbcb95e93a8" :topic/math-academy-id 4821 :topic/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff8459b-90ba-55e5-9f4c-e7dc1bde3386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472952} {:answer/id #uuid "69b91f43-8ffb-569f-b70b-a21938ee98c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69b91f43-8ffb-569f-b70b-a21938ee98c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472953} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ef4810-6e2e-50ca-bc69-366ef2ac37ff" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a80a966-8115-55b3-b207-094cf3b39558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472954} {:answer/id #uuid "c7175560-155a-5f70-8923-4994865bc4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a80a966-8115-55b3-b207-094cf3b39558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472954} :answer-field/id #uuid "2700e44f-bd91-59e9-97e3-31bcc